---
import DocLayout from '../../layouts/DocLayout.astro';

const sections: [string, string][] = [['need', 'What you need'], ['systems', 'macOS or Linux'], ['steps', 'Steps']];
---
<DocLayout title="Get started" description="Set up Mob: install mobd on your office computer (macOS or Linux), configure Codex, Claude Code or Pi, and pair Mob on your laptop or phone." lead="About ten minutes: install mobd on the computer that does the work, tell it which agents to run, then pair your laptop or phone." sections={sections}>
  <section id="need">
    <h2>What you need</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>An office computer</strong> that stays on and online, running mobd:
        <ul>
          <li><strong>macOS 13</strong> or later on <strong>Apple Silicon</strong>, with a user logged in.</li>
          <li><strong>Linux:</strong> Ubuntu 22.04 or 24.04, or Debian 12 or 13, on amd64 or arm64. A server or VM without a screen is fine. Other distributions build mobd from source.</li>
          <li>Windows is planned.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>An agent</strong> installed and set up on it (its account or an API key): <a href="https://developers.openai.com/codex/cli/" rel="noreferrer">Codex</a>, <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/quickstart" rel="noreferrer">Claude Code</a> or <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent" rel="noreferrer">Pi</a>, or several.</li>
      <li><strong>A device with Mob:</strong> <a href="/download/#mac">Mob Desktop</a> for macOS (Windows and Linux on the way), or Mob Mobile for <a href="/download/#ios">iPhone and iPad</a> (iOS 17+) or <a href="/download/#android">Android</a> 8.0+.</li>
      <li><strong>A way for the device to reach the computer:</strong> the same network or VPN, or <a href="/remote/">remote access</a> through Tailscale or Cloudflare.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="systems">
    <h2>macOS or Linux</h2>
    <p>Mob works the same from your devices either way. On the office computer the two differ in how the agent gets a screen:</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">macOS</th><th scope="col">Linux</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">The agent's screen</th><td>the Mac's own screen, shared by all conversations</td><td>a virtual desktop per conversation</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Turns at once</th><td>one; the others wait in a queue</td><td>up to four, side by side (<code>maxRunning</code>)</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Permissions</th><td>Screen Recording and Accessibility</td><td>none</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Runs as</th><td>a LaunchAgent of the logged-in user</td><td>a systemd user service; no login needed</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Live view</th><td>the Mac's screen</td><td>the open conversation's desktop</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>More in <a href="/desktops/">Desktops &amp; live view</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="steps">
    <h2>Steps</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Install mobd:</strong> <a href="/install/macos/">on macOS</a> or <a href="/install/linux/">on Linux</a>. Each guide ends with mobd running and checked.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/configure/">Configure agents</a>:</strong> choose Codex, Claude Code and Pi, and the folder they work in.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/pair/">Pair a device</a>:</strong> run <code>mobd link</code> and scan the code in Mob.</li>
      <li><strong>Optional, <a href="/remote/">remote access</a>:</strong> reach the office computer from anywhere.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Then start a conversation and try something simple, such as “Look at the screen and tell me what you see.” Stuck? See <a href="/faq/">FAQ &amp; help</a>.</p>
  </section>
</DocLayout>
